{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1786559800,"nonce":"RzxC67sVPuC7RHZcSq4GBdecJk0=","message":"An implementation of this was built and then BACKED OUT, because review found the safety argument does not hold. Recording it so the next attempt does not rediscover it.\n\n**What was built.** `oto replay --batch \u003cid\u003e` -\u003e `ingestion/service.Replay`: one transaction doing `Get` -\u003e status gate (`failed`/`partial`) -\u003e horizon gate -\u003e `Reopen` (compare-and-set to `pending`) -\u003e enqueue onto the accept path's own outbox. No contract change, no migration. The compare-and-set was verified correct under concurrency, and `Locate` was verified tenant-safe.\n\n**Why it was backed out.** The horizon gate guards the wrong axis. It refuses a batch older than `alert_event_keys` retention, on the theory that surviving dedupe keys make a re-append a no-op. But dedupe keys are OCCURRENCE-scoped (`internal/alerts/domain/lifecycle.go:715-718`, `\"occ:\"+o.id+\":opened\"`) and a `failed` batch committed nothing, so it holds ZERO keys at whatever age. The gate protects a set that is already empty for exactly the batches replay exists to help.\n\n**The harm, traced.** A batch fails Monday carrying `firing`. A later batch resolves the alert Tuesday. The operator replays Monday's batch Thursday. `selectRule` (`lifecycle.go:540-548`) sees a `firing` observation on a terminal occurrence and takes `refireAfterGrace` -\u003e T7, and `internal/alerts/service/lifecycle.go:448` mints `occID := id.New()`. The dedupe key is keyed to that brand-new id, so it is a guaranteed miss: a new occurrence, a new `occurrence.opened`, and a fresh `notify.evaluate`. Someone is paged for an incident that closed two days earlier. `process.go:206` stamps `ObservedAt: now`, and nothing compares it to anything -- `SourceUpdatedAt` is stored (`lifecycle.go:605-607`) and never read back.\n\n**A fix that was considered and rejected.** Stamping `ObservedAt` with the batch's own `received_at` instead of `now` looks like the bounded fix and is worse: `withinRefireGrace` compares `RecordedAt` to `endedAt + grace`, and a failed batch's `received_at` almost always precedes the observation that resolved the occurrence, so the comparison becomes true by construction and forces T8 (reopen) every time. It converts a probabilistic reopen into a deterministic one.\n\n**What a correct implementation needs.** Gate on SUPERSESSION, not age: refuse the replay if any alert the batch would act on has an occurrence that has moved since the batch's `received_at`. Age is irrelevant; being overtaken by a later batch is the whole risk.\n\n**Two further findings.**\n- `internal/ingestion/repository/rejections.go:36-40` has no `ON CONFLICT` and mints a fresh id per row, so every replay duplicates that batch's rejection rows into the feed added by 5dde27d. A batch with 40 rejections replayed twice shows 120.\n- Any test must seed a ZERO-WRITE failed batch and resolve the alert between passes. The integration test written here processed the batch to completion and only then marked it failed, which is the shape replay does not need to worry about -- it could never have caught this.","files":null}]}